# When will the world's population peak, and at what size?
subtitle: "A host quizzes a co-host and you: when does the world's population peak, and how big? Then see the number that has already peaked."
tags: ["geography","data"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"The peak is coming\" note \"When?\""

## When will the world's population peak, and at what size?
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1950
      - 1960
      - 1970
      - 1980
      - 1990
      - 2000
      - 2010
      - 2020
      - 2024
      - 2030
      - 2040
      - 2050
      - 2060
      - 2070
      - 2080
      - 2084
      - 2090
      - 2100
    series:
      - {name: Past, values: [2.49, 3.02, 3.69, 4.45, 5.33, 6.17, 7.02, 7.89, 8.16, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null]}
      - {name: UN projection, values: [null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, 8.16, 8.57, 9.18, 9.66, 9.99, 10.19, 10.28, 10.29, 10.27, 10.18]}
    xlim:
      - 1950
      - 2100
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 12
    y_label: Billions of people
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 420
      w: 860
      h: 200
    label_size: 30
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: Population, including UN projections
      authors: Our World in Data, from UN World Population Prospects 2024
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/population-with-un-projections
      finding: 8.16 billion in 2024; medium projection peaks at 10.29 billion in 2084, 10.18 billion in 2100
    - id: peakchild
      title: The world has passed "peak child"
      authors: Our World in Data
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/the-world-has-passed-peak-child
      finding: the number of children under five peaked in 2017 (about 698 million); under-15s peaked around 2020
    - id: wpp
      title: World Population Prospects 2024
      authors: United Nations, Population Division
      year: 2024
      finding: 'medium projection: peak of about 10.3 billion in the mid-2080s'

    scale yr cites ["owid"] x 90 y 200 width 820 min 2020 max 2200 value 2084 ticks 9 hidden true
    dot peak_pt #b5482e at.data [2084,10.29] hidden true
    text peak_l "Peak: 2084, 10.3 bn" #b5482e at.data [2040,11.3] cites ["owid"] font_size 44 hidden true
    text fill_l "kids grow up, live longer" #2f6b8f at.data [2048,5.2] font_size 40 hidden true
    text now_l "8.2 bn" #b5482e at.data [1985,8.8] cites ["owid"] font_size 52 hidden true

    card title "When will the world's population peak?"

A: When will the world's population stop growing? Since 1950 it has more than tripled, to eight point two billion. Your guess, and my co-host's.
    draw axes line_1 now_l

B: Easy, I'll go first: it never stops. Twenty-two hundred, at the earliest.
    draw yr

    ask "Click on the line where you think the peak comes." on yr store y default "2120" tolerance 0.06
        right: Close. The UN expects the peak in the 2080s.
        wrong: Not quite. Look where the UN puts the peak.

A: Sorry. The UN's projection says 2084.
    highlight yr

B: Fine. But at the top it'll be huge. Fifteen billion?
    erase yr

    hide axes line_1 now_l

    ask "And how many people at the peak? Drag the slider." estimate.min 8 estimate.max 16 estimate.value 10.3 estimate.unit billion store p default "12" relative true tolerance 0.06
        right: Close. A little over ten billion.
        wrong: The UN's figure is about ten billion.

    erase estimate_1

    show axes line_1 now_l

    animate box.x 70 box.y 195 box.w 860 box.h 430 duration 2

A: Ten point three, so two billion more than today. After the peak, a slow decline.
    draw line_2 peak_pt peak_l

B: But people have fewer kids now. Why does it keep growing?
    highlight line_2

A: They do. In fact the number of children under five already peaked, back in 2017.
    path kid_tick dashed thick #2f6b8f points [[2017,0],[2017,1.4]] data true
    text kid_l "2017: peak child" #2f6b8f at.data [2040,2.2] cites ["peakchild"] font_size 44

B: Wait. Fewer babies, but more people?
    highlight kid_l

A: Yes: today's children still have to grow up, and more of us live to old age. The total catches up later.
    draw fill_l

B: So the babies peaked first, and the rest of us follow in 2084. I was off by a century.
    highlight peak_pt effect circle

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail

    path t_hill smooth #2f6b8f points [[120,260],[300,380],[480,500],[620,560],[700,570],[780,560],[880,530]] style.stroke_width 9 hidden true
    icon t_flag size 140 set twemoji x 700 y 640 icon_look picture of "triangular flag" hidden true
    text t_q "20??" #b5482e x 330 y 600 font_size 130 hidden true
    thumb t_band "When do we peak?" kind band hidden true

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail

    text t2_num "8,200,000,000" #2f6b8f x 560 y 420 font_size 96 hidden true
    text t2_up "+ ? more" #b5482e x 640 y 280 font_size 110 hidden true
    thumb t2_band "How many of us, at most?" kind burst hidden true

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail

    icon t3_a size 260 set twemoji x 270 y 440 icon_look picture of "man raising hand" hidden true
    icon t3_b size 240 set twemoji x 720 y 440 icon_look picture of "thinking face" hidden true
    text t3_say "\"It never stops!\"" #3d3833 x 500 y 640 font_size 64 hidden true
    thumb t3_band "Is he right?" kind band hidden true
